# ── credentials ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing under api_key/ is ever committed. The loaders in code/llm_client.py read these files at
# runtime and fall back to environment variables; there is no key in tracked source.
api_key/
*.key
.env
CLAUDE.local.md

# ── licensed source data ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# UMLS requires a UTS licence and its agreement restricts redistribution. cui_to_tui.csv is a
# 443,700-row dump of UMLS CUI→semantic-type mappings, so publishing it would redistribute
# licensed content. Regenerate it from your own licensed Neo4j instance — see README.
cui_to_tui.csv
cui_with_terms.csv
*_umls_*.csv

# ── large artefacts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# cache/ (1.6 GB) and results/ (257 MB) stay out: cache/ is embeddings and per-disease duration
# lookups that rebuild from Neo4j and the LLM, results/ is reader output that anyone can reproduce
# from frozen/ with one script.
#
# frozen/ is NOT in this list. "Regenerable" was the wrong call for it: rebuilding a frozen file
# needs a licensed UMLS Neo4j instance plus hours of LLM retrieval, so in practice it is not
# reproducible at all without the original setup. Shipping it (90 MB, largest file 7.2 MB) is what
# lets someone re-run the reader, try a different prompt, or check a number without any of that.
cache/
results/
logs/
prompt_dumps/
oneshot/
*.pkl
*.pt
*.zip
*.jsonl.bak*

# datasets/ is tracked in full — benchmark.json plus the seeds / symptoms / durations /
# query_entities that Stage A consumes. Those inputs are LLM-generated per dataset, so a clone
# without them cannot reproduce retrieval even with a working Neo4j.
#
# _raw_* stay out: they are download caches, not sources. build_new_dataset.py fetches each one
# over the network when missing (MedBullets CSV, MedMCQA and MMLU via the HF datasets-server), so
# shipping 4 MB of someone else's corpus adds nothing a clone cannot fetch itself.
datasets/_raw_*

# ── python ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.venv/
venv/

# ── os ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store

# ── sweep artefacts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One file per (top_k, λ, μ, mode, …) combination — 30+ per dataset, all regenerable in seconds
# from pool/ via render_from_pool.py. Only the two variants promoted to first-class methods
# (walker_criticality, walker_adaptive) are tracked, under the same naming as walker/walker_interval.
frozen/*/walker__k*.json
frozen/*/*__random10.json

# pool/ IS tracked. It is the substrate the whole utility iteration runs on: every candidate the
# walker retrieved, with its raw cos / bc / hop / role / path. Without it a change to the ranking
# means a 25-minute Neo4j re-walk per dataset; with it, seconds. 102 MB is worth that.
#
# datasets/*/criticality.json IS tracked: it is a per-QUESTION signal, and a per-question signal is
# what someone needs to make the weights vary by question instead of being constants. The scripts
# that consumed it are a separate line of work and stay out; the data it produced does not depend
# on them.
datasets/*/answer_type.json
judged_utility.py
verify_judge.py
extract_answer_type.py
extract_duration_criticality.py
# 103 MB of retrieved candidate pools. Regenerable from Neo4j (~25 min/dataset) and not needed to
# reproduce a frozen prompt, which is already tracked.
pool/
# -- local helper scripts ------------------------------------------------------
# Convenience runners restored locally from the README workflow. Keep them out
# of git until the canonical runner/report story is cleaned up.
run_reader_vllm_batch.py
summarize_vllm_runs.py
